    <string name="aiding_wasted">Heard for nothing: %1$s. The device does not know where those satellites move, which is what resetting the satellite data puts right.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_title">Why a position is rejected</string>
    <string name="reason_help_intro">The device holds every position against several rules and rejects it as soon as the first one speaks. A rejection is not a fault: where the signal is spoofed there are more rejected answers than accepted ones, and that is the work being done properly.</string>

    <string name="reason_help_sign_width">The device named the radius it is somewhere inside, and the radius is over half a kilometre.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_width">Such an answer names a district rather than a place. It is what the towers give while the device is indoors.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_speed">Reaching this place from the last one in the time between them would have taken over 200 km/h.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_speed">One of the two is false. A spoof throws the marker far off rather than easing it along.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_contradiction">Two different positions arrived carrying the same moment.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_contradiction">The source is contradicting itself, which is what happens when a receiver is caught between a transmitter on the ground and the satellites.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_false_place">The answer landed in an area you marked as somewhere the spoof puts devices.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_false_place">A transmitter puts the marker there, not the satellites. The areas belong to the group, so marking one from any device is enough.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_unmoved">The movement sensors say the device was not carried anywhere, and the position changed.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_unmoved">Only a false signal moves the marker of a device lying still: steps and journeys are counted by a chip of their own that a transmitter cannot reach.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_drifted">The device is lying where it was put down, and the answers walk away from it a little at a time.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_drifted">A slow drift. A step of thirty metres never trips a comparison between neighbours, but within an hour the marker is somewhere else, so the distance is counted from where the device was put down.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_hurried">The device has not been touched, and the answer says it is travelling at the speed of a car.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_hurried">The receiver reports a speed of its own alongside every position, and on a device lying still that speed is zero. A false signal draws a road and a speed along it, and the speed is inside the single answer — so this catches the first false position rather than the one after it.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_contradicted">The position from the satellites and the position from the towers and the Wi-Fi are kilometres apart.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_contradicted">The towers are harder to imitate than the satellites. This is the one rule that does not compare an answer with an earlier one, so it catches both a slow drift and the very first false answer.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_circling">The answers go round a closed circle at a steady speed.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_circling">Every stretch of such a track reads as ordinary driving. What gives it away is that the road closes, which is how a transmitter draws one and not how anybody drives.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_repeating">The device has been through this same run of places before.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_repeating">A recorded false route is being played through again.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_one_band">The fix rests on the satellites of the 1575 MHz band — GPS, Galileo, BeiDou — while GLONASS is heard and left out of it.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_one_band">A spoofing transmitter works in one band. GLONASS broadcasts at 1602 MHz, out of its reach, so the receiver could not reconcile it with the satellites it was handed.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_one_transmitter">Every satellite arrives at the same strength however low it claims to be, and the far band is silent.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_one_transmitter">A real sky grows quieter the lower the satellite. One strength for all of them means one point on the ground.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_time_apart">Between two epochs the time the satellites carry advanced by a different amount than the device\'s own clock did.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_time_apart">The device counts the seconds since it was switched on, and nothing transmitted can reach that count. A signal built to move the device has to move the time it carries, and the step shows up here.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_off_orbit">Seen from where the towers and Wi-Fi place the device, a satellite it hears is below the horizon, or its signal arrived hundreds of kilometres sooner or later than its distance allows.</string>
    <string name="orbits_roaming_title">Download satellite data while roaming?</string>
    <string name="orbits_roaming_text">1.2 MB. Without it the spoofing check against satellite orbits does not work.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_off_orbit">Where every satellite is at every moment is published daily by stations around the world, out of reach of anything near the device. Signals that do not fit those orbits were made up for some other place.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_settling">The first five minutes after the device was switched on, while the answer rests on fewer than four satellites.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_settling">In those minutes the receiver reports places the device has never been. Answers of that window are held to tighter limits: wider than a hundred metres, or faster than 5 m/s, and they are rejected.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_sign_by_source">You switched this source of a position off in the settings.</string>
    <string name="reason_help_means_by_source">Nothing is wrong with the answer: it is collected and logged, but it moves no marker and reaches nobody. That is what the switch is for.</string>

    <string name="aiding_title">Reset the satellite data</string>
    <string name="aiding_explained">The device remembers where the satellites move and where it was last, to find its place sooner. When that goes wrong, satellites are seen but left out of the position. A reset puts it right.</string>
    <string name="aiding_forget">Reset</string>
    <string name="aiding_keep">Leave it</string>
    <string name="aiding_erased">Reset done</string>
    <string name="aiding_refused">The receiver would not</string>
    <string name="aiding_confirm_title">Reset the satellite data?</string>
    <string name="aiding_confirm_text">The first position takes a few minutes instead of seconds. Worth doing when satellites are seen for days but the position stays rough.</string>

